{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>הִנְנִי מַמְטִיר לָכֶם</b> · Ibn Ezra said (on Exodus 16:4) that because it came down in the likeness of rain from the heavens, it is called מַמְטִיר, and we find \"יַמְטֵר עַל רְשָׁעִים פַּחִים אֵשׁ וְגָפְרִית\" (Psalms 11:6), \"וַה' הִמְטִיר עַל סְדֹם וְעַל עֲמֹרָה גָּפְרִית וָאֵשׁ\" (Genesis 19:24). And perhaps they came together with the rain. And the opinion of Onkelos (on Exodus 16:4) is that it is an expression of descent: \"הָא אֲנָא מַחֵית.\" And it is possible that it is from the matter of \"כַּמַּטָּרָא לַחֵץ\" (Lamentations 3:12), even though that is a different root, for every projectile that comes from above they say thus, and it is called \"מַטָּרָה\" on account of the arrows one casts (מַמְטִיר) upon it, and they said (Psalms 78:27) \"וַיַּמְטֵר עֲלֵיהֶם כֶּעָפָר שְׂאֵר וּכְחוֹל יַמִּים עוֹף כָּנָף.\" Or perhaps it says thus concerning the fowl of the heavens because it came down upon them like rain." "<b>לֶחֶם</b> · Because they would make bread from it, as it is written (Numbers 11:8) \"וְעָשׂוּ אֹתוֹ עֻגֹת,\" for every food is called לֶחֶם, not only when it is from wheat or barley. And it says <b>מַמְטִיר לֶחֶם</b>, because in order for it to be bread He casts it down to them, and so too \"אֶרֶץ מִמֶּנָּה יֵצֵא לָחֶם\" (Job 28:5), and so too \"לְהוֹצִיא לֶחֶם מִן הָאָרֶץ\" (Psalms 104:14) — that it brings forth wheat from which they make bread. And there are those who say (Ibn Ezra, and likewise Radak here and in Sefer ha-Shorashim, root לחם) <b>הִנְנִי מַמְטִיר לָכֶם לֶחֶם</b> — food, and so too \"לֶחֶם אִשֶּׁה\" (Leviticus 3:16), \"כִּי אֶת לֶחֶם אֱלֹהֶיךָ הוּא מַקְרִיב\" (Leviticus 21:8), \"כִּי תֵשֵׁב לִלְחוֹם אֶת מוֹשֵׁל\" (Proverbs 23:1) — all of them mean food. But the correct interpretation is that \"לִלְחוֹם\" (there) means to eat bread, and \"לֶחֶם אֱלֹהֶיךָ\" (Leviticus 21:7–8) is an epithet, that it is a meal for Elohim like bread is for a man, for we find \"לֹא עַל הַלֶּחֶם לְבַדּוֹ יִחְיֶה הָאָדָם\" (Deuteronomy 8:3)." "<b>לְמַעַן אֲנַסֶּנּוּ הֲיֵלֵךְ בְּתוֹרָתִי</b> · If they keep the commandments dependent on it, that they not leave over from it nor go out on the Sabbath to gather — the language of Rashi. And this is not correct, but rather it is as He said (Deuteronomy 8:16) \"הַמַּאֲכִלְךָ מָן בַּמִּדְבָּר אֲשֶׁר לֹא יָדְעוּן אֲבֹתֶיךָ לְמַעַן עַנֹּתְךָ וּלְמַעַן נַסֹּתְךָ לְהֵיטִיבְךָ בְּאַחֲרִיתֶךָ,\" for it was a trial for them in that they had no food in their possession, and they could see no recourse in the wilderness except the manna, which they did not know from the outset and had not heard of from their fathers, and a thing descended for them day by day, and they would hunger for it, and despite all this they consented to follow after HaShem into the wilderness, a non-place of bread. And thus He further said to them (Deuteronomy 8:2) \"וְזָכַרְתָּ אֶת כָּל הַדֶּרֶךְ אֲשֶׁר הוֹלִיכֲךָ ה' אֱלֹהֶיךָ זֶה אַרְבָּעִים שָׁנָה בַּמִּדְבָּר לְמַעַן עַנֹּתְךָ וּלְמַעַן נַסֹּתְךָ לָדַעַת אֶת אֲשֶׁר בִּלְבָבְךָ הֲתִשְׁמֹר מִצְוֺתָיו אִם לֹא,\" for He could have led them on the road of the cities around them, but He led them through a wilderness of snake, serpent, and scorpion (Deuteronomy 8:15), and they would have no bread except from the heavens day by day, in order to test them and to do them good in the end, that they might believe in Him forever. And I have already explained the matter of the test in the verse \"וְהָאֱלֹהִים נִסָּה אֶת אַבְרָהָם\" (Genesis 22:1). And the Rav said in the Guide for the Perplexed (III:24) — to make known to everyone who knows, and to test whether there is benefit in the worship of El, and whether it involves a deficiency or not. And if so, it should have said \"לְמַעַן יְנַסֶּה לָדַעַת.\" Now, He mentioned nothing here except the manna, which is the bread that He cast down to them, but when Moses said to them (Exodus 16:8) <b>בְּתֵת ה' לָכֶם בָּעֶרֶב בָּשָׂר לֶאֱכֹל וְלֶחֶם בַּבֹּקֶר לִשְׂבֹּעַ</b>, we know that everything was told to him, but Scripture shortens in matters repeated in the context of a command or a narrative, as I have reminded you many times (above 9:12, 10:12, 11:1), and sometimes it does not mention one of them at all, as it is written in this portion (Exodus 16:32) <b>זֶה הַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה ה' מְלֹא הָעֹמֶר מִמֶּנּוּ</b>, and the command was not written at all, and so in many places. And according to the opinion of those who say (Ibn Ezra) that \"לֶחֶם\" means all food, it is possible that <b>הִנְנִי מַמְטִיר לָכֶם לֶחֶם</b> refers to both the manna and the quail, for He would give them what they asked — they asked for bread and meat — and Moses explained that the meat would be in the evening to eat and the bread in the morning to be satisfied, in the manner of the whole world."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {2 "The Guide reference is to III:24. The Hebrew המשכיל יבין does not appear in this passage; the kabbalistic tag is not invoked here."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:09:44.689861639Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}